# Platform probe budget-burn prototype

This external prototype is not installed product code or an admission runner.
It must run only inside an allocated Linux amd64 resource window, using the
existing shared locks and their actual inherited descriptors. The admitting
parent owns the resource checks, port snapshot, deadline and custody until the
namespace and all its children have exited. This prototype opens no lock and
must never be launched beside an unadmitted compiler or native service.

Use Python 3.9 or newer from the existing host and the checksum-qualified
VictoriaMetrics 1.155.0 binary. No package installation or download is needed.
The two source files are `observe-probe-slo.py` and `probe-slo.metricsql`.

The admitted namespace must have only loopback, no default route and a private
proc mount, with Python as PID 1. Bring loopback up before replacing the
namespace shell with Python. Within that existing admitted caller, the final
command is:

```sh
exec python3 /absolute/path/observe-probe-slo.py \
  --metrics-binary /absolute/path/to/victoria-metrics-prod \
  --output /absolute/fresh/private/output-directory
```

The source refuses a mismatching native checksum, redirects and an existing
output path. It uses ephemeral IPv4 loopback ports and disables HTTP proxies.
Its 120-second bound includes two sequential store instances, each with
GOMAXPROCS=2 and a 256 MB cache budget. Both stores receive termination and are
reaped; SIGTERM and SIGINT enter the same cleanup path. A forced shutdown is a
failure. Keep the resulting private logs and store directories for inspection;
the prototype never deletes preexisting data.

The first instance checks exact production windows at one fixed historical
query time, including explicitly observed failure zeros and an empty store.
It verifies native input series before evaluating the expression, requires the
exact expected firing set and projects only site_id and endpoint. Multiple burn
windows produce one result for their shared endpoint.

The second instance uses an explicit 10,000-node inventory calculation: five
cells with two platform readiness endpoints per cell. It imports one day of
40 series and 115,200 samples from four selected metric families, verifies the native sample counts, and times
twenty queries requesting nocache=1. One endpoint burns quickly. It stops the
store before measuring regular data files' apparent and allocated bytes.
Report that synthetic shape and quiet-store scope exactly. It does not create
10,000 endpoint probes, simulate node requests, exercise concurrent ingestion
or establish performance against the existing 17-million-series workload.
Additional collector self-metrics and other telemetry are outside this shape.

The measured signal is this private observer's binary readiness outcome. It
does not define a request SLO. Coverage below 90 percent, stale data, nonbinary
samples or duplicate endpoint series are unknown and cannot establish healthy
service. Telemetry loss needs the separate pipeline-health capability.

Passing native queries does not qualify live vmalert/Alertmanager delivery,
installer wiring, server authorization, user-facing SLO selection or OBS-111.
Those remain separate product requirements. The prototype adds no telemetry
database in PostgreSQL, model, dataset or dependency.
